=== design_hash refrozen, 2026-08-18 (AMB-123 round 14) ===
The committed design_hash moved, deliberately:

  sha256:b2a4c088018511439166f0c214d70de0546739886191067b27686f4b421cd419
  sha256:8d056aa1c1f5075974ed5cd6cc1f4be4ffa826c4851c9b1f70bcbc73c3ef2330

Not one byte of the design changed. Two defects in the profile did.

1. Clause 7 pinned integral floats and then said "the shortest form that
   round-trips" for everything else, which is not a specification -- it is
   whichever spelling the host language uses. Python pads a one-digit exponent
   (1e-07 against JavaScript's 1e-7) and switches to exponential below 1e-4
   where JavaScript switches below 1e-6 (1e-05 against 0.00001). That is the
   whole nF/uF/nA/us band expressed in base SI. An auditor wrote a conforming
   implementation in six lines of JavaScript, expressed one 100 nF capacitor
   as 1e-7 F -- which netlist-ir.schema.json invites by saying "the compiler
   normalizes units" -- and got a different design_hash for a document both
   implementations accept. Clause 7 now specifies ECMA-262 Number::toString
   and the self-test pins eleven values against `node -e JSON.stringify`.

2. design_hash covered header.generator and header.source_hash, so an
   unchanged netlist got a new content address every time the compiler was
   rebuilt, and another one for a comment-only edit to the DSL source. Both
   are blanked out of the preimage now, alongside design_hash itself.

The refreeze was verified by re-running the auditor's own reproduction: the
independent JavaScript implementation and this gate now agree on the committed
example (8d056aa1...) and on the base-SI variant (8aaee4da...).

The transcript above is the current one; both summary lines in it were updated
in this commit rather than re-quoted down here. check-layout.sh reads only the
first occurrence of each, so a second copy appended below would be the stale
one nobody reads -- which is the failure that gate was written for, and which
it caught me committing while writing this very section.

=== benchmark goldens join both gates, 2026-10-22 ===
The document count above went from 1 to 3 and the self-test from 62 to 71
checks, in the commit that added them. The two new documents are the
elaborator's output for the living benchmark sources,
benchmarks/blinker-555/blinker-555.ir.json and
benchmarks/esp32s3-devboard/esp32s3-devboard.ir.json, each with its source
map, compiled with the benchmark directory as the compile root. For those the
gate resolves source paths in that directory, requires the sources to exist,
and verifies source_hash; the three "unverified" lines above are all about
ir/examples, whose sources were never committed. rhoform/tests/
test_benchmarks.py holds each golden to what its source elaborates to, byte
for byte.

The span-overlap rule changed with them: two identities may now share one
declaration span exactly (a module instantiated twice declares each copy of a
member with the same statement, as source-map.schema.json already said),
while two different spans over the same bytes still fail. The self-test case
that planted an identical span as its overlap now plants a partial one, and
new cases pin both directions.

=== benchmark (b) joins, 2026-10-22 ===
The document count went from 3 to 4 when benchmarks/buck-3v3/buck-3v3.rhoform
became a living source (a component-level proposal pending EE review,
AMB-128) with its IR and source map beside it. The PASS line and the schemas
rows above were updated in that commit, for the reason the round-14 section
gives. The third "unverified" line was reworded on the same branch: the spec
now pins the source_hash serialization, so the example's value is unverified
because it is a placeholder written before that rule, not because no rule
exists.

=== the standard library joins, 2026-10-22 ===
The benchmark goldens now list the standard library modules each source
names, as rhoform/lib/<module>.rhoform (spec 04, "The standard library";
E-8). Those files are the toolchain's, not the benchmark's, so the gate
resolves a path under rhoform/lib/ against the repository root instead of
the benchmark directory, and checks their bytes there. One self-test case
pins that (72 checks, up from 71): a planted library module whose bytes
moved under its recorded sha256 is caught in the toolchain's tree.

=== IR version 1, 2026-10-22 ===
The syntax 0.2 line had widened Net.name's pattern in
netlist-ir.schema.json in place, so that a net named from an array
member's path (cells[2]/OUT, N_leds[0].a) would validate, and had
edited the source-map schema's files[].path description the same way
for the standard library. Schema files are immutable per version
(README, "Versioning policy"), so both edits moved into new files,
netlist-ir.v1.schema.json and source-map.v1.schema.json, and the
version 0 files were restored byte for byte. The compiler now writes
ir_version 1 and sourcemap_version 1.

The schemas gate routes each document by the version it declares: the
rows above show the examples and negative controls against the version
0 schemas and the goldens against version 1 (conformance expectations,
not transcribed here, are version 1 too), and a document declaring no
version or an unknown one fails. Every benchmark golden and conformance
expectation was regenerated through the compiler; each differs from its
predecessor only in its version fields and design_hash, which moved
because ir_version is hashed. Neither ir-hashes summary line changed:
the examples are still version 0 and the gate's checks do not depend on
the version.

=== IR version 2, unfrozen, 2026-10-24 ===
Syntax 0.3 opened, unfrozen (decision OD8), and with it IR version 2:
netlist-ir.v2.schema.json and source-map.v2.schema.json, new files, with
the version 0 and version 1 files left byte for byte as they were. Version
2 adds the required `interfaces` (empty until syntax 0.3's interfaces land)
and `annotations` arrays, a component's `rules`, a net's `isolated`
(present only when true) and authored `ground_kind`, and an assertion's
`severity` (present only when "warning"); it widens Port.name and
PortRef.port to member paths and removes Assertion.waiver. It is unfrozen:
it may change in place until L5b freezes it with syntax 0.3, and no tagged
release ships while it is (README, "Unfrozen working versions"). The
compiler writes ir_version 2 and sourcemap_version 2.

The transcript above was re-run for this change and replaces the one
before it, for the reason the round-14 section gives. The schemas rows now
list the two version 2 schema files and eight version 2 negative controls,
n10-n17, each a minimal version 2 document with one defect at the location
it declares (69 controls in all, up from 61, which is the gate's new
floor). The goldens validate against version 2. The ir-hashes self-test
went from 72 to 78 checks: the version 2 sort rules (interfaces by
instance and port, annotations and each component's rules by their
canonical bytes) and the interface node-coverage rule each have a case.
The ir-hashes PASS line did not change: the examples are still version 0,
and the gate checks the benchmark goldens whatever their version.

Every benchmark golden and conformance expectation was regenerated once,
through the compiler. The preservation oracle in rhoform/tests/test_ir_v2.py
removes the version 2 additions from each, sets ir_version to 1, and
recomputes design_hash: every one equals the version 1 design_hash it had
before this change (24 documents: three benchmarks, sixteen elaboration
cases, five package cases).

=== interfaces fill IR version 2, 2026-10-24 ===
Syntax 0.3's interfaces (T1, L2a) landed, and version 2's `interfaces`
array now holds one entry per bundle node: each element of a module's
bundle port, each component view, and each bundle nested in either. Version
2 is unfrozen, so its schema changed in place: an entry's `members` must not
be empty, since every interface has at least one leaf. Two version 2
negative controls pin the new shape, n18 (an entry with no member) and n19
(an unset parameter written as null, where it is omitted), for 74 controls
in all, up from 72. The ir-hashes self-test went from 78 to 79 checks: the
schema's order on an interface's `members`, by member path, which nothing
held before (the L1a review's IR2-5), now has a sort rule and a case.

The transcript above was re-run for this change and replaces the one before
it. The ir-hashes PASS line did not change, and no benchmark golden moved:
the benchmarks use no interface, so their IR is byte for byte what it was.
The documents that do hold interfaces are the new conformance expectations
(four elaboration cases and one package case), which the schemas gate
validates against version 2 with every other expectation.
